INT. SCHOOL BAND ROOM - AFTER HOURS

A cramped practice room. Gray carpet, acoustic foam on the walls, a battered upright piano, music stands knocked askew. A single lamp glows. On the shelf, a row of dusty trophies. Rain on the high window.

JUBILEE ADEBAYO-MORROW (16, braces, oversize cardigan, a trombone case at her feet) perches on the piano bench, picking at a loose thread.

Across the room, HOLDEN "HOLLY" PRZYBYLSKI (17, drum sticks tucked behind one ear, band jacket, a skateboard under one arm) leans in the doorway.

HOLLY
They said you quit.

JUBILEE
I didn't quit.

HOLLY
They said it at assembly. Mr. Dunphy said it.

JUBILEE
Mr. Dunphy says a lot of things.

HOLLY
He said you were "taking a break."

JUBILEE
It's a break.

HOLLY
From what?

JUBILEE
From being second chair.

HOLLY
You're not second chair.

JUBILEE
I'm second to Ingrid.

HOLLY
Ingrid has a private tutor.

JUBILEE
Ingrid has a private everything.

HOLLY
So what?

JUBILEE
So I can't win.

HOLLY
Jubilee.

JUBILEE
What?

HOLLY
It's a trombone, not a war.

JUBILEE
(sharply)
You don't understand.

HOLLY
Then explain.

JUBILEE
It's not that I'm not good. It's that I'm good and it doesn't matter.

HOLLY
It matters to me.

JUBILEE
You're a drummer.

HOLLY
I'm a drummer who listens.

JUBILEE
To what?

HOLLY
To you. Every Tuesday. Through the wall.

JUBILEE
That's creepy.

HOLLY
It's the practice room next door.

JUBILEE
It's still creepy.

HOLLY
It's the best thing I hear all week.

A phone rings, muffled, from inside Holly's jacket. He fishes it out, glances at the screen.

HOLLY (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Hey, Mom. No. I'm still at school. No, I'm fine.

MOM (V.O.)
(tinny)
Holden, it's nine o'clock. Where are you?

HOLLY
(into phone)
Band room. Helping a friend.

MOM (V.O.)
Which friend?

HOLLY
(into phone)
Jubilee.

MOM (V.O.)
The trombone girl?

HOLLY
(into phone)
Mom.

MOM (V.O.)
I'm only asking.

HOLLY
(into phone)
I'll be home soon.

He hangs up. Pockets the phone. Jubilee is watching him.

JUBILEE
She knows about me?

HOLLY
Everyone knows about you.

JUBILEE
What do they know?

HOLLY
That you're really good. And that you're pretending you're not.

JUBILEE
I'm not pretending.

HOLLY
Okay.

JUBILEE
I'm not.

HOLLY
Jubilee, play something.

JUBILEE
Now?

HOLLY
Now.

JUBILEE
There's nobody here.

HOLLY
I'm here.

A long silence. Rain on the window. Jubilee looks at the case, then at him.

JUBILEE
What do you want to hear?

HOLLY
Whatever you were going to play before you quit.

She snaps the latches open. Lifts the trombone. Slides the slide out, slow, like a drawn breath.

JUBILEE
It's not finished.

HOLLY
Neither am I.

She raises the horn. He crosses the room, sits on an upturned bucket, and pulls out his sticks. He taps the lid of the piano once, softly, counting her in.
